{
  "requirement": "The `habeas` CLI MUST use `clap` to define and parse its commands and options.",
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "structural: The only structural obligation in `requirements.cli.clap` is: \"The `habeas` CLI MUST use `clap` to define and parse its commands and options.\" A hand-written argument parser with the same behavior would violate it, so it is a real structural obligation.\n\nBoth roots meet it:\n- `src/main.rs#struct[Cli]` is `#[derive(Parser)]` with `#[command(name = \"habeas\")]` and a `#[command(subcommand)]` field. This defines the CLI with clap.\n- `src/main.rs#fn[main]` calls `Cli::parse()`. This is where clap parses the arguments. It also uses clap's `Cli::command().error(...)` to report usage errors.\n\nThe other command definitions are reached from `Cli`, so they need no marker. `src/main.rs#enum[Command]`, `src/main.rs#enum[MdbookCommand]` and `src/main.rs#enum[HookCommand]` all derive `clap::Subcommand`. `Command` also holds the option definitions, including the `Author` fields.\n\n`main` also has an early branch for the `habeas-rustc` executable name. It runs `habeas::execution_coverage::compile_process` before clap parsing. This is a separate internal compile-wrapper mode, not the `habeas` command-line interface, so it does not conflict with the clause.\n\nbehavioral: `requirements.cli.clap` says only that the `habeas` CLI MUST use `clap` to define and parse its commands and options. That is an implementation choice (a prescribed library), so it has no independent observable behavioral obligation that needs test evidence.\n\nThe marked tests still check clap-style behavior, which supports the requirement:\n- `tests/cli.rs#fn[clap_defines_commands_options_and_usage_errors]` checks the clap-formatted `--help` usage line and the listed commands, and that hidden commands are absent from help. It checks the `status` options, the `author-evidence` alias, and exit code 2 with clap-style messages plus `Usage: habeas` for an unknown argument, a missing required argument and conflicting arguments.\n- `tests/coverage.rs#fn[obsolete_status_coverage_flag_is_rejected_with_usage]` checks that `status --coverage` exits with code 2 and prints the clap-style `unexpected argument` error and `Usage:`.\n\nNo behavioral gap or missing marker was found.",
  "references": "ffdf09f9b71cb4dae5ece568926183048aeda88fa93ae0ef4b72087262bb8063",
