        "status": "success",
        "rationale": "The only structural obligation in `requirements.cli.clap` is: \"The `habeas` CLI MUST use `clap` to define and parse its commands and options.\" A hand-written argument parser with the same behavior would violate it, so it is a real structural obligation.\n\nBoth roots meet it:\n- `src/main.rs#struct[Cli]` is `#[derive(Parser)]` with `#[command(name = \"habeas\")]` and a `#[command(subcommand)]` field. This defines the CLI with clap.\n- `src/main.rs#fn[main]` calls `Cli::parse()`. This is where clap parses the arguments. It also uses clap's `Cli::command().error(...)` to report usage errors.\n\nThe other command definitions are reached from `Cli`, so they need no marker. `src/main.rs#enum[Command]`, `src/main.rs#enum[MdbookCommand]` and `src/main.rs#enum[HookCommand]` all derive `clap::Subcommand`. `Command` also holds the option definitions, including the `Author` fields.\n\n`main` also has an early branch for the `habeas-rustc` executable name. It runs `habeas::execution_coverage::compile_process` before clap parsing. This is a separate internal compile-wrapper mode, not the `habeas` command-line interface, so it does not conflict with the clause."
